WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.905
SPEAKER: Welcome back
to another episode

00:00:01.905 --> 00:00:04.059
of "BigQuery Spotlight."

00:00:04.059 --> 00:00:07.210
Today, we're talking about
BigQuery data governance

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:09.510
so that everyone in
your organization

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:12.270
can easily find and
leverage the data they

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:14.490
need to make
effective decisions,

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.980
while minimizing overall
risk and ensuring

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:18.930
regulatory compliance.

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:20.678
Let's jump in.

00:00:20.678 --> 00:00:24.670
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:30.590
The term data governance is
thrown around quite a bit,

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:33.230
but what does it actually mean?

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:35.150
Data governance
is everything you

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:39.740
do to ensure your data is
secure, private, accurate,

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:43.610
available, and usable
inside of BigQuery.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:47.750
For us, governance starts
during data onboarding.

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:51.080
Let's say your team has
received a new request.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:53.090
Someone from your
e-commerce department

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:57.570
wants to add transactions data
into a new BigQuery table.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.810
The first step in
ensuring governance

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:01.890
is understanding the data.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:05.239
For example, figuring out
where the data is coming from

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:08.180
and what information
it contains.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:09.980
In this case, the
transactions will

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:13.190
be extracted from a
database, and you already

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:14.820
know the schema.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.920
So let's start by creating
a new blank BigQuery table.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.740
Next, you'll need to make
sure that sensitive data is

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:22.970
protected.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:25.970
To do so, you'll want to
categorize the information

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:27.770
in your new table.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:30.170
For example, marking
specific columns

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:34.010
as having personally
identifiable information.

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:37.190
You might remember that
tables can have labels.

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:39.890
While labels are a great
way to easily categorize

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:42.740
BigQuery objects, they
are a bit limited,

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:45.710
and can't be used
for specific columns.

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:48.620
Lucky for us, BigQuery
has deep integrations

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:51.710
with Data Catalog, Google
Cloud's data discovery

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:54.560
and metadata management service.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.920
Data Catalog automatically
tracks technical metadata

00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:59.850
for BigQuery assets.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:03.650
So we can already find
our new BigQuery table,

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:06.920
and see things like column
names, descriptions and when

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:08.600
it was created.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:10.340
But the really cool
part is that you

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:13.850
can create schematized tags
that act as annotations

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:16.070
to capture metadata.

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:18.440
We've already created
a tag template

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:21.440
that tracks data
governance information, so

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:26.140
now let's fill it in and attach
the tag to the Email column.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:26.770
Great.

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:29.920
Now we're tracking that this
entity contains customer email

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.270
addresses.

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:34.450
But you might also notice
there's a Comments column

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:36.610
that stores freeform
text customers have

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:38.800
added to their order.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.570
There's a chance that
some of these rows

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:43.310
contain more
sensitive information.

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:46.150
This is where DLP comes in.

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:48.820
DLP, or Cloud Data
Loss Prevention,

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:50.620
is Google Cloud's
service designed

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:55.630
to help you discover and protect
your most sensitive data.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:59.170
With DLP, you can create an
inspection job that scans

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:01.690
comments for email addresses.

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:05.350
DLP can scan data that lives
in Data Store, Google Cloud

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:08.830
Storage, or BigQuery,
and can send you

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:12.310
an email with the scan results,
save the results of that

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:15.790
to a table inside of BigQuery,
or even write the results

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:18.940
directly into Data Catalog.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:22.210
Many BigQuery users choose
to leverage an orchestration

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:24.640
tool, like Cloud
Composer, to build

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:26.800
data onboarding pipelines.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:29.830
For example, you might
load your transactions data

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:33.160
in a locked down staging
project inside of BigQuery

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:37.480
and kick off a DLP scan
when new comments are added.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:40.810
The results will be listed in
the staging table's metadata

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:43.180
inside of Data Catalog.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:46.360
If the scan shows that any
sensitive information is

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:48.880
included in a comment,
then you can also

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:51.970
kick off a D
identification workflow,

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:55.270
where DLP replaces
PII with something

00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:58.450
like an asterisk
or a hash symbol.

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:01.990
Then, the new data is finally
appended to the production

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:04.400
BigQuery table.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.910
Now that you've de-identified
and classified your data,

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:11.750
you're ready to design
access policies.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:14.600
To understand data
access in BigQuery,

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:17.779
you need to familiarize
yourself with Identity Access

00:04:17.779 --> 00:04:20.630
Management, or IAM.

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:24.500
If you haven't already watched
our video on BigQuery IAM,

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:27.200
I recommend pausing
and taking a look.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.160
We've linked it in
the comments below.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:33.250
Great, so on to data sharing.

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:36.190
When it comes to granting
access to BigQuery data,

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:38.350
many administrators
choose to grant

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:41.950
Google Groups representing your
company's different departments

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:46.000
access to specific data
sets or projects so policies

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.430
are simple to manage.

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:51.220
Say you have a project that
stores your organization's

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:53.140
e-commerce data.

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:56.380
You might give the e-commerce
team the BigQuery Data Viewer

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:58.280
role in this project.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:00.490
Now, if someone on
the e-commerce team

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:02.500
needs data from
a different team,

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:04.960
like the product
development department,

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:06.820
you may be inclined
to just grant them

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:09.490
access to that data set.

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:12.550
Instead, you could use
an authorized view,

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:16.510
or an authorized UDF, so that
your e-commerce analyst can

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:19.000
get limited access
to product data

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:22.000
while still keeping more
manageable team to project

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.930
permissions.

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:27.400
So this all sounds great, but
what about those PII columns

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:30.310
that we flagged before
in Data Catalog?

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:32.470
You definitely don't
want the e-com team

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:35.920
to have access to those, unless
they have special clearance

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:37.720
from security.

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:39.940
With BigQuery, you
can control access

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:44.890
to specific columns using
policy tags from Data Catalog.

00:05:44.890 --> 00:05:47.560
Here, we've created
a taxonomy, which

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:50.380
classifies the level of
clearance needed to access

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:52.600
different types of data.

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:56.020
Anyone who needs access to
our highly sensitive data

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:58.540
should be added as a
fine-grained reader

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:00.380
to the high resource.

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:05.410
So in our case, we'll just add
the e-com PII clearance group.

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:08.110
Next, we can apply
these as policy tags

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:10.720
to columns in our
BigQuery table,

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:15.250
marking the columns with
PII as highly sensitive.

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:17.770
And to take it one
step further, maybe you

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:20.470
don't want all e-commerce
folks to get access

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:22.900
to the transactions data.

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:25.390
Instead, each
analyst should only

00:06:25.390 --> 00:06:27.910
be able to view
transactions for the product

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:30.250
category they work on.

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:32.680
You can control access
to specific rows

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:35.950
in BigQuery using a
row-level access policy,

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:38.710
like the one we're showing here.

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:41.560
Between storing data
in different containers

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:45.640
and leveraging authorized
views, UDFs, policy tags,

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:47.830
and row-level
access policies, you

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:50.140
have a lot of options
for sharing data

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:52.000
in a secure manner.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.910
Check out the documentation
below for some more guidance

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:57.400
on pros and cons
and general best

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:00.880
practices for policy access.

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:05.140
OK, so we've onboarded our
data set and we've shared it.

00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:08.360
The last step we'll talk
about is ongoing monitoring,

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:11.800
including ensuring
that data is accurate.

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:14.710
This is where data
quality comes in.

00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:16.180
There are lots of
different tools

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:18.700
that allow you to declare
quality validation

00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:23.470
tests by writing custom
assertions using SQL.

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:26.530
For example, you may want
to check and make sure

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:28.630
that you don't have
any duplicate entries

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:30.820
in your table.

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:33.370
We've actually created
an open source framework

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:35.680
for data quality
validation that's

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:37.900
included in the comments below.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:40.420
And keep an eye
out for Data Form,

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:43.090
our tool for developing
SQL pipelines,

00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:46.750
which will be open to
new customers soon.

00:07:46.750 --> 00:07:48.520
Just like we
mentioned before, you

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:51.760
can use an orchestration
tool like Cloud Composer

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:53.980
to programmatically
run these tests

00:07:53.980 --> 00:07:56.860
and alert you if
something seems wrong.

00:07:56.860 --> 00:08:00.310
You can also use the Data
Catalog API to make sure

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:03.010
that quality metadata
is added to tags

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:06.070
and is easily discoverable.

00:08:06.070 --> 00:08:08.050
Aside from monitoring
quality, you

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:10.930
might also want to monitor
who's accessing the data

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:12.850
and how they're using it.

00:08:12.850 --> 00:08:14.260
Well, you're in luck.

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:17.410
In a few weeks, we're diving
into monitoring your BigQuery

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:20.170
deployment, so be
sure to tune in.

00:08:20.170 --> 00:08:22.600
And remember, stay curious.

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:26.250
[MUSIC PLAYING]

